WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.315
You now know how to run all six conversations.

00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:09.870
The next question is: how do you fit them into real life?

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.726
There are two common ways.

00:00:11.776 --> 00:00:13.941
The first is one workshop day.

00:00:13.991 --> 00:00:18.746
You go through all six conversations in one day, often away from the office.

00:00:18.796 --> 00:00:22.086
It builds energy and a strong shared experience.

00:00:22.136 --> 00:00:27.483
But it’s intense, and the later conversations often get less attention than they deserve.

00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:29.522
The second way is a series.

00:00:29.572 --> 00:00:34.585
One conversation every week or two, in sessions of ninety to a hundred and twenty minutes.

00:00:34.635 --> 00:00:41.647
It fits better into a normal calendar, gives people time to reflect between sessions, and lets you test agreements before the next one.

00:00:41.697 --> 00:00:44.090
For most teams, this is what I recommend.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:45.347
You can also mix them.

00:00:45.397 --> 00:00:50.961
For example, start with Why and Who in a half day, and then do the others as shorter sessions.

00:00:51.011 --> 00:00:55.055
Whatever you choose, start with the conversation your team needs most.

00:00:55.105 --> 00:00:56.668
The health check helps you here.

00:00:56.718 --> 00:00:59.428
For a new team, that’s usually Why and Who.

00:00:59.478 --> 00:01:02.749
For an existing team, it’s often Decisions or Values.

00:01:02.799 --> 00:01:05.079
A typical series could look like this.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.253
Week one: Why are we here?

00:01:07.303 --> 00:01:09.445
Week two or three: Who are we?

00:01:09.495 --> 00:01:13.303
Then Rhythm, Decisions and Values, each two weeks apart.

00:01:13.353 --> 00:01:16.956
And Evolve after about three months, to look back at everything.

00:01:17.006 --> 00:01:21.745
And plan the next session before you end the current one. Momentum is fragile.

00:01:21.795 --> 00:01:25.825
If the next date isn’t in the calendar when people leave the room, it often doesn’t happen.

